==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/tɪl/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*tila-}}, whence also {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|til}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|zil}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Ziel}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|tilr}}, {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐍄𐌹𐌻}}.

====Adjective====
{{head|ang|adjective}}

# [[good]] {{gloss|morally good; competent; useful, etc.}}
#: {{usex|'''Til''' sceal mid '''tilum'''.|t=The good shall be with the good.|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 2===
Possibly from {{etyl|non|ang}} {{m|non|til}}<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|till}}</ref> tho the OED has it as "Germanic" and related to {{etyl|non|-}} and to {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|til}}<ref>{{R:OED|till}}</ref> <!--http://oxforddictionaries.com/definition/till-->

====Preposition====
{{head|ang|preposition}}

# [[to]], [[until]], [[unto]]
#: {{usex|Ðá cueð '''til''' him ðe Hǽlend|t=Then quoth unto them the Savior.|lang=ang}}

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|n|tilas}}

# use, service, convenience
#: {{usex|Gewritu secgaþ ðæt seó wiht sý mid moncynne miclum '''ticlum''' sweotol and gesýne, sundorcræft hafaþ.|t=Writings say that those beings were to mankind of great service; they clearly and obviously had special powers.|lang=ang}}
# goodness, kindness
#: {{usex|Me on ðínum '''tile''' gelǽr ðæt ic teala cunne ðín sóðfæst weorc healdan.|t=Thy goodness teaches me that I should maintain your righteous work.|lang=ang}}

